      "period": "classical",
      "gloss": "turn everything upside down, throw everything into confusion",
      "evidence": [
        "Sall. Cat. 10.1: <span lang=\"la\">saevīre fortūna ac miscēre omnia coepit</span> (Lewis &amp; Short s.v. <span lang=\"la\">saeviō</span>)"
      ],
      "comment": "<span lang=\"la\">Miscēre</span>, to mix, is also to throw into disorder; in the passive, <span lang=\"la\">omnia mixta</span>."
    },
    {
      "latin": "sūrsum deorsum",
      "status": "alternative",
      "period": "modern",
      "gloss": "topsy-turvy, upside down (in disorder)",
      "grammar": "two adverbs, “upwards, downwards”",
      "evidence": [
        "Traupman 110: <span lang=\"la\">omnia erant sūrsum deorsum</span>",
        "Traupman 396, 401"
      ],
      "comment": "Classically the pair means “up and down, to and fro”, of movement (see up and down). Lewis &amp; Short gives no instance of it for things in disorder, Traupman’s sense; there <span lang=\"la\">omnia</span> is the subject: “everything was up and down”."
    }
